1. Definitions

"Software" means the files in the ee/ directory of the HexProcure repository,
in source or object form, and any copy, modification or derivative work of
them. "Feature" means a feature id listed in the feature map in ee/features.ts
in the release named in Your Order or, if none is named, in the latest release
at the Order date. "Tier" means the team or business tier named in that map;
the business Tier covers every team Feature. "Licence Key" means a signed
token issued by the Licensor that names a Tier, You as licensee and a validity
period; it is valid while the licence check in the HexProcure code accepts it.
"Licence Check" means the code in the HexProcure repository that verifies a
Licence Key. "Order" means the order that You sign or confirm in writing,
including by electronic means, against the Licensor's quote, that identifies
You and under which the Licensor issues a Licence Key. "Production Use" means
any use of the Software other than evaluation, development, testing or
contribution, including use for Your own operations or for Your customers.
Running HexProcure with every paid Feature off is not Production Use of the
Software and needs no Licence Key. "You" means the person who accepts this
licence; for Production Use, "You" means the legal entity, public body,
non-profit organisation or entrepreneur named in the Order, and the individual
who signs the Order confirms that they can bind it and is not the licensee.
"Affiliate" means an entity that controls You, that You control, or that is
under common control with You, where control means more than half of the
voting rights; Affiliates named in the Order may use the Licence Key as You.
Use of the Software by a contractor only to operate it for You or a named
Affiliate is Your use.

2. Who may accept this licence, and how

The free grant in section 3 and the contribution licence in section 7 are open
to any person. Production Use under section 4 is offered to legal entities,
public bodies, non-profit organisations, and entrepreneurs within the meaning
of Sections 420(1) and 421 of Act No. 89/2012 Coll., the Czech Civil Code. It
is not offered to consumers. By copying, modifying or using the Software You
accept this licence; where You act for an organisation, You confirm that You
are authorised to bind it. This is an offer to an indeterminate circle of
persons that is accepted by that conduct, without notice to the Licensor
(Section 2373 of the Civil Code). The licence for Production Use is concluded
through Your Order, as section 4 describes. If You are a consumer, sections 8,
9, 10 and 11 apply only to the extent the mandatory law of Your habitual
residence allows.

3. Free grant: view, modify, test and contribute

The Licensor grants You a non-exclusive, territorially unlimited, free of
charge licence to view, copy, modify and test the Software, to distribute it
and make it available to the public as section 6 permits, to publish patches
to it, and to send contributions to the Licensor, for the purposes of
evaluation, development, testing and contribution. This grant needs no Licence
Key and carries no remuneration. It lasts for the whole duration of the
economic rights to the Software unless it ends under section 8. For as long as
a licence under this section or section 4 is in force, the Licensor also
grants You a licence under patent claims it can license that are necessarily
infringed by using the Software as the Licensor provides it, for that use
only; this patent licence ends if You sue the Licensor for patent infringement
relating to the Software. This licence gives You no rights other than those it
states.

4. Production Use needs a Licence Key

For the validity period of Your Licence Key, the Licensor grants You a
non-exclusive licence to install, run and back up the Software on Your own
systems, including failover systems, for Production Use of each Feature that
Your Tier covers, within the limits stated in the Licence Key and the Order.
You obtain a Licence Key through an Order that identifies You. By placing the
Order You accept this licence, including sections 8, 9, 10 and 11, expressly
and in writing. The remuneration for Production Use is the fee stated in the
Order. A numeric or named limit in the Licence Key binds You only if the Order
states what is counted, the period and the fee if You go over; going over such
a limit is a contract matter under section 10 and not copyright infringement.
A trial on live data is Production Use unless the Order calls it an evaluation
and sets an end date no later than 30 days after it starts. A Licence Key is
issued to the licensee named in it and may not be shared, lent, resold or
offered to third parties other than named Affiliates and contractors as
section 1 describes. It passes to a legal successor, or with a transfer of
Your business or part of it, only with the Licensor's written consent
(Sections 2365 and 2383 of the Civil Code), which the Licensor will not refuse
unless the successor would offer the Software as a hosted service or fees are
unpaid. Production Use with no Licence Key where You have never held one, or
with a forged Licence Key, is not licensed and infringes the Licensor's
copyright; section 10 does not limit the Licensor's claims for such use. Use
during a stop period under section 8, and between expiry or revocation of a
Licence Key and the Licensor's notice, only to stop and to export Your data,
is allowed and is not infringement.

5. No circumvention

You may not make Production Use of the Software together with a Licence Check
that has been moved, changed, disabled, weakened or worked around, and in
Production Use You may not remove or obscure any functionality in the Software
that the Licence Key protects. You may not generate, forge, modify or extend a
Licence Key. You may test the Licence Check, including in a security
assessment, on a system that is not in Production Use. You may not remove or
obscure a notice that identifies the Software as licensed. Nothing in this
licence restricts a right that the law grants a lawful user of a computer
program and that cannot be narrowed by agreement, such as error correction, a
backup copy, study of the program's principles and decompilation for
interoperability under Section 66(1) and (6) of Act No. 121/2000 Coll., the
Czech Copyright Act.

6. Redistribution, notices and trademarks

You may not sell, sublicense, rent or distribute the Software, or a product or
service of which the Software is the main thing being sold, as a standalone
offering. You may not let a third party, other than a named Affiliate, use the
Software as a customer of a hosted or managed service that You sell, and You
may not offer a service whose main function the Software provides to third
parties in that way. You may run the Software on systems a contractor operates
for You, and You may use it to carry out procurement for Your own business and
for named Affiliates. You may distribute copies that keep this licence and all
notices intact, such as a fork made for contribution or an internal copy for
Your own deployment. You must ensure that anyone who gets a copy from You also
gets a copy of these terms. If You modify the Software, You must state in
modified copies that You have modified it. This licence gives You no right to
use the names HexProcure or HEXENKRAFT, or their logos, except to state where
the Software comes from.

7. Contributions

If You send a contribution to the Licensor for inclusion in the Software, You
grant the Licensor a non-exclusive, irrevocable, territorially unlimited, free
of charge licence to use that contribution in all known ways of use, for the
whole duration of the economic rights to it. This includes the right to
modify, combine and distribute the contribution, to grant sublicences, and to
make it available to third parties under this licence or under other terms.
The licence is irrevocable and lasts for the whole duration of the economic
rights because each contribution becomes part of a published code base that
others rely on, which is a serious reason within Section 2000 of the Civil
Code. You keep the copyright in Your contribution and in any modification You
do not send to the Licensor. Your moral rights are not waived; You consent to
changes to the contribution and to its publication with or without Your name
(Section 11(2) and (3) of the Copyright Act). The Developer Certificate of
Origin in CONTRIBUTING.md certifies origin; for a file in ee/, the licence
indicated in the file is this licence, which is not an open source licence. By
sending the contribution You confirm that You hold the rights needed to grant
this licence. A public fork that is not sent to the Licensor is not a
contribution.

8. Term and termination

This licence runs until terminated. It ends automatically if You generate,
forge, modify or extend a Licence Key. If You make Production Use and have
never held a Licence Key, or make Production Use together with a Licence Check
that has been moved, changed, disabled, weakened or worked around, or Your
Licence Key expires or is revoked, or You use a Feature outside the Tier that
covers it, You must stop that use within 14 days after the Licensor's written
notice; if You do not, the Licensor may withdraw from section 4 and the Order
by notice, and the free grant in section 3 continues. For any other breach,
the Licensor may withdraw from this licence and revoke the Licence Key by
notice if You do not cure the breach within 30 days after the Licensor's
written notice. The Licensor may also withdraw by notice if You fail to pay an
undisputed fee in the Order within 30 days after a written reminder.
Withdrawal has effect only for the future. On termination of Production Use
You stop Production Use and keep Your data. You may keep one copy of the
Software for 90 days to export Your data, and for longer where the law
requires You to keep records, and You may use that copy only to read those
records. Fees for the period before termination are not refunded where
termination follows Your breach; fees prepaid for the period after termination
are refunded. Sections 1, 5, 7, 9, 10, 11 and 12, the prohibitions, notice
duties and trademark sentence in section 6, and the duties in this section
survive termination.

9. Warranty and defects

The Software is provided "as is". The Licensor gives no warranty of
merchantability, fitness for a particular purpose, non-infringement,
uninterrupted or error-free operation, or that Your own procurement process
meets the laws that apply to You. Under the free grant in section 3 You have
no rights from defective performance beyond those that mandatory law attaches
to gratuitous performance. For Production Use, You waive in Your Order, to the
extent Section 1916(2) of the Civil Code permits, Your rights from defective
performance, including those under Sections 2389c and 2389d of the Civil Code,
except the rights in the next two sentences. The Licensor will offer a
correction of a security defect in the Software within 30 days after Your
written notice, and a correction of any other material defect within a
reasonable time. If it does not, You may claim a proportionate refund of the
fee for the affected period. The Licensor has no other duty to supply updates.

10. Limitation of liability

To the extent permitted by law, the Licensor's total liability arising out of
or in connection with the Software is limited to the fees You paid to the
Licensor for the Software in the twelve months before the event giving rise to
the claim. The Licensor is not liable for indirect, incidental or
consequential loss, lost profits, loss of data, fines or regulatory penalties.
The cap, and the exclusion of loss of data and of fines, do not apply to a
claim that the Software as the Licensor provided it infringes a third party's
copyright or trade secret, or to a fine caused by a defect the Licensor
confirmed and did not correct under section 9; for those claims the Licensor's
total liability is limited to twice the fees You paid in the twelve months
before the event giving rise to the claim. The Order may require the Licensor
to defend an infringement claim. Nothing in this licence limits liability that
cannot be limited by law, in particular for harm caused intentionally or by
gross negligence, harm to a person's natural rights, or harm to a weaker party
(Section 2898 of the Civil Code).

11. Governing law and courts

This licence and any dispute arising out of it are governed by the laws of the
Czech Republic, excluding its conflict of law rules. The United Nations
Convention on Contracts for the International Sale of Goods does not apply.
For Production Use, the court competent for the Licensor's registered seat has
jurisdiction, as agreed in Your Order; You may also sue the Licensor in the
courts of Your seat, and the Licensor may sue You in the courts of Your seat.
The Order may instead name an arbitral institution seated in the Czech
Republic. If You are a consumer, the courts that the law of Your habitual
residence makes competent decide. This licence is written in English and the
English text governs.

12. General

This licence, together with the Order under which a Licence Key was issued, is
the whole agreement about the Software. The Order may add support terms, named
Affiliates, limits, a data-processing arrangement, an intellectual-property
indemnity and, where You are a public body, the clauses that public
procurement law requires; if the Order and this licence conflict, the Order
prevails, and Czech law under section 11 still governs. If a part is
unenforceable, the rest stays in force and the unenforceable part is replaced
by the valid rule that comes closest to its purpose. Where You are an
entrepreneur, the parties agree that Sections 1799 and 1800 of the Civil Code
(adhesion contracts) do not apply, as Section 1801 permits between
entrepreneurs. Unless You are a consumer, You consent in advance to the
Licensor assigning this licence to a legal successor or to an entity that
acquires the HexProcure business (Sections 1895 and 1897(1) of the Civil
Code); the assignment takes effect towards You when the Licensor emails it to
the address in Your Order or, for the free grant, publishes it in the
HexProcure repository. If the assignee offers the Software as a hosted
service, You may end Production Use on 30 days' written notice and fees
prepaid for the unused period are refunded. Notices under this licence are in
writing; email is writing. A notice to You goes to the email address in Your
Order; a notice to a person without an Order may be given by publication in
the HexProcure repository. A notice to the Licensor goes to the address stated
in the Order or, if none is stated, to the address at the end of this licence;
the Licensor may publish a replacement address in the HexProcure repository,
and notices then go there. A notice takes effect on the next business day
after it is sent. The Licensor may publish a new version of this licence; it
applies to releases of the Software published after it. A Licence Key stays on
the version accepted in the Order until the key expires, including if You
install a later release; You move to a new version for Production Use only by
a new Order.

